"Praise be to Allah; He revealed the Book to His servant. This Book is upright, containing no crookedness. Its purpose of revelation is to warn the disbelievers of a severe punishment in the Hereafter and to give good tidings to the believers who do righteous deeds that they will have a fair reward. The believers will abide in Paradise forever.\n\nAmong those who are warned are also those who ascribe children to Allah: the Arab polytheists consider the angels to be daughters of Allah, the Jews say Ezra (Uzayr) is the son of Allah, and the Christians say Jesus is the son of Allah. The Christians are the most persistent in this matter. Yet neither they nor their forefathers possess any knowledge regarding this claim; they merely imitate. This is a grave utterance coming out of their mouths and a monumental slander against Allah.\n\nO My Messenger! You would almost consume yourself with grief because those who do not believe in this Book might perish. Do not grieve over them; carry out your duty. For We have made everything on the earth an adornment in order to test people: which of them will perform the best deeds? In the end, We will turn those adornments into dry, barren soil. One must not be deceived by the transient adornments of the world, but must turn toward what is eternal.\n\nThe story of the Companions of the Cave (Ashab al-Kahf) and al-Raqim is not a greater miracle than the other miracles of Allah. Those youths were valiant young men who believed in their Lord. Allah increased them in guidance. Rising up against the idolatrous king, they declared tawhid (the oneness of God): 'Our Lord is the Lord of the heavens and the earth; we will never call upon any deity besides Him.' Their people took deities besides Allah; they have no clear proof. The wrongdoers are those who invent lies against Allah.\n\nThe youths separated themselves from their people and their idols and took refuge in the cave. They implored Allah: 'Our Lord, grant us mercy from Yourself and prepare for us a right way out in our affair.' Thereupon, Allah caused them to sleep for years and blocked the entrance of the cave. When they awoke, they could not tell how long they had stayed; they said a day or less. Some left off disputing, saying, 'Your Lord knows best.' They sent one of their number to the city to buy food, cautioning him to be discreet and not let anyone become aware of them.\n\nAllah caused the people of the city to discover them so that they might know that the Hereafter is true. The people disputed about building a structure over the cave; the believers, however, decided to build a place of worship (masjid). Disputes arose concerning the number of the Companions of the Cave: figures such as three, five, and seven were suggested. It is Allah who knows them best; you should not enter into deep disputes regarding this matter.\n\nDo not say concerning the future, 'I will do such-and-such tomorrow,' except [by adding], 'If Allah wills.' When you forget, remember Allah and say, 'I hope my Lord will guide me to what is right.' They remained in their cave for three hundred years and added nine more. Say: 'Allah knows best how long they remained. To Him belongs the unseen of the heavens and the earth. How well He sees, and how perfectly He hears! They have no protector besides Him, and He associates no one in His judgment.'\n\nElmalılı conveys various views regarding the length of time the Companions of the Cave stayed in the cave: 300 years according to solar years, and 309 years according to lunar year calculation. While some exegetes state that this duration is Allah's own explanation, others point out that it is the statement of those who disputed over the number. Elmalılı emphasizes that this period represents the duration during which the supporters of the Companions of the Cave remained in hiding until achieving victory, and that it indicates that Islam would achieve victory in a shorter time.\n\nIn verses 26–31, it is recalled that only Allah knows the duration of the Companions of the Cave, that His words cannot be altered, and that there is no refuge other than Him. Believers are commanded to be patient alongside those who implore their Lord morning and evening, and not to turn away from them seduced by the adornments of the world. It is explained that once the truth has arrived, whoever wills may believe and whoever wills may disbelieve; that the punishment of Hell has been prepared for the wrongdoers; and that those who believe and do righteous deeds will be rewarded in the Gardens of Eden ('Adn).\n\nIn verses 32–44, the story of the two men is recounted: one of them was given two vineyards of all kinds of grapes, surrounded with date palms, with a river flowing between them. The man who was superior in wealth and manpower claimed that his vineyard would never perish and that the Hour would not come. His companion, however, warned him that he should not disbelieve in Allah, who created him from dust and then from a drop of fluid. In the end, the arrogant man's wealth is destroyed, his vineyard collapses upon its trellises, and he feels remorse, saying, 'Would that I had not associated anyone with my Lord!'\n\nIn verses 45–53, the life of this world is likened to water sent down from the sky; the vegetation intermingles, then turns into dry debris which the winds scatter. Wealth and children are the adornment of worldly life, whereas enduring good deeds are better in terms of reward and hope. On the Day of Judgment, mountains are made to move, the earth becomes completely flat, and everyone is presented before their Lord in ranks. The records of deeds are laid open, leaving nothing uncounted. Furthermore, it is recalled that Iblis refused to prostrate to Adam, that he was of the jinn, and that his offspring should not be taken as allies.\n\nIn verses 54–59, it is stated that people are contentious in most things, and that what prevents them from believing when a prophet comes is nothing other than waiting for the fate of past nations to befall them. Prophets are sent only as bearers of glad tidings and warners. There is none more unjust than those who associate partners with Allah and turn away even when admonished with the verses. Allah places coverings over their hearts and deafness in their ears. Your Lord's mercy is vast; He does not punish immediately, but grants respite until an appointed term. The cities that committed injustice have been destroyed.\n\nIn verses 60–66, the journey of Moses (Musa) with his young companion Joshua son of Nun (Yusha' b. Nun) to the junction of the two seas is recounted. When they forgot their fish and took shelter by a rock, the fish made its way into the sea and disappeared. Upon this, Moses realized that this was the place they were seeking and retraced his steps. There, they found a servant of Ours upon whom We had bestowed mercy and knowledge from Our presence (al-Khidr). Moses wished to follow him, but al-Khidr told him that he would not be able to have patience. This story is cited as evidence that esoteric divine knowledge (al-'ilm al-ladunni) cannot be acquired through effort alone and that hidden knowledge (al-batin) truly exists.\n\nRushd (spiritual maturity and right guidance) is finding what is good and remaining on the right path. In this statement, there is an indication of the necessity of humility before a scholar, that the primary aim of acquiring knowledge is to attain rushd, and that modesty, proper manners (adab), courtesy, devotion, and service are prerequisites for learning knowledge. By telling Moses, \"You will never be able to bear with me in companionship,\" al-Khidr revealed his insight into Moses's psychological state and indicated that through this request Moses would receive a lesson in patience. Moses replied, \"If Allah wills, you will find me patient, and I will not disobey you in any matter.\" Al-Khidr then stipulated the condition, \"If you follow me, do not ask me about anything until I mention its explanation to you.\"\n\nThe two set out and boarded a ship. Al-Khidr scuttled the ship by making a hole in it. Moses said: \"Did you scuttle it in order to drown its passengers? You have certainly done a dreadful thing!\" The reason for rendering the ship defective was that ahead of them was a king who seized every sound ship by force. Al-Khidr damaged the ship to prevent this king from confiscating it. This was an act of assistance under the principle of choosing the lesser of two evils.\n\nAfterwards, they encountered a boy, and al-Khidr killed him. Moses (Musa) said: \"Did you kill an innocent soul without [it being in retaliation for] a soul?\" The status of the boy has been debated in the commentaries: while the majority (jumhur) say he was a child who had not yet reached puberty, some narrations report that he was a young man of twenty years of age. Al-Khidr explained that the boy's parents were believers, and it was feared that the child would burden them with transgression and disbelief, and so it was desired that Allah grant them in exchange one better and more merciful. If the youth was a mature disbeliever, his killing might be in accordance with the Shariah; however, if he was a child, does this situation create a discord between the Shariah (religious law) and haqiqah (inner truth)? As Imam Rabbani also stated, the Shariah and haqiqah are one and the same; the fact that al-Khidr was commanded through special revelation explains this difference.\n\nFinally, they reached the people of a town. The people of the town refused to host them as guests. There they found a wall on the verge of collapsing, and al-Khidr restored it. Moses said: \"If you had wished, you could surely have taken a wage for it.\" Al-Khidr stated that beneath the wall was a treasure belonging to two orphan boys and that their father had been a righteous man; therefore, his Lord willed that those children should reach maturity and extract their treasure. Al-Khidr indicated that he did not do all these things of his own accord, but by the command of Allah. The story demonstrates that things which appear harmful on the surface are beneficial when their inner reality is known, and that there is no contradiction between the Shariah and haqiqah. Moses raised no further objection, and the parting between them took place.\n\nAfter this story, it is reported that Dhu'l-Qarnayn was asked about. According to some narrations, those who posed the question were the polytheists, and according to others, the People of the Book. Dhu'l-Qarnayn is an epithet meaning possessor of two horns or possessor of the east and the west. There are varying opinions regarding who he was: some identify him as Sa'b or Shammar Yur'ish from among the Himyarite rulers of Yemen, while others say he was Afridun or Alexander. It is understood that the figure mentioned in the Qur'an was an eminent personage possessing the station of prophethood. Historical debates aside, it is emphasized that Dhu'l-Qarnayn was a ruler renowned for justice and faith.\n\nElmalılı transmits various narrations regarding the identity of Dhu'l-Qarnayn. Some say that he was one of the rulers of Yemen, specifically Afrikish, titled Dhu'l-Manar, or an individual from his lineage. However, historical records are mixed; a connection is also made with the name Shammar. Elmalılı notes that amidst these debates, the soundest verdict is the statement narrated from Ali: \"Dhu'l-Qarnayn was a righteous servant; he loved Allah, and Allah loved him.\" According to him, the style of the Qur'an also aims not so much to specify names and identities as to recount the narrative concerning his qualities.\n\nDhu'l-Qarnayn was granted power on earth and the means to attain everything. First, he directed his way to the west and reached the setting place of the sun. There, he saw the sun as if setting in a dark muddy spring or a hot spring. This sight serves as a reminder that worldly grandeur is limited and transient. He found a people there, and Allah gave him authority either to punish them or to treat them with kindness. Dhu'l-Qarnayn, on the other hand, promised punishment to whoever commits wrong, and a fine reward to whoever believes.\n\nHaving completed his affairs in the west, Dhu'l-Qarnayn set out this time towards the east. He reached the rising place of the sun and found a people for whom He had not provided any shelter against the sun. This was an uncovered community possessing neither buildings nor clothing. In contrast, Dhu'l-Qarnayn possessed countless means and resources; yet this people had none of them. Allah encompassed everything he had with His knowledge.\n\nThen Dhu'l-Qarnayn took a path toward the north and reached a place between two mountains. There, he encountered a people who could scarcely understand speech. Stating that Gog and Magog (Ya'juj and Ma'juj) were causing corruption in the earth, this people offered tribute to Dhu'l-Qarnayn so that he would build a barrier between them. Elmalılı relates that there are narrations indicating this people were the Turks. Dhu'l-Qarnayn, however, told them that the wealth Allah had granted him was better, and rather than material aid, he requested strength and manpower support.\n\nDhu'l-Qarnayn had blocks of iron brought, leveled the two sides, and melted the iron by blowing upon the fire with bellows. He poured molten copper over it and built a sturdy barrier. This barrier became an obstacle that Gog and Magog could neither scale nor pierce. Elmalılı emphasizes that this process was realized not through ordinary matter alone, but through a divine power. Dhu'l-Qarnayn stated that this barrier was a mercy from Allah, but that it would be destroyed when the appointed time of the Day of Resurrection arrived.\n\nElmalılı points out that the construction method and solidity of the barrier do not match historical structures such as the Great Wall of China or the Iron Gate (Demirkapı). According to him, this barrier represents both a material and spiritual obstacle; perhaps it was a unity and order formed by the people (the Turks) who assisted Dhu'l-Qarnayn. The collapse of this barrier is a sign of the emergence of Gog and Magog and of the Day of Resurrection. Furthermore, some Sufis have interpreted this story as a symbol of man's psychological and spiritual journey.\n\nWhen Dhu'l-Qarnayn reached between the two barriers, he found a people who could understand almost nothing. Saying that Gog and Magog were causing corruption in the earth, they asked Dhu'l-Qarnayn to build a barrier, offering him their wealth in return. Dhu'l-Qarnayn, however, asked them for help with strength and exertion, instructing them to bring blocks of iron. When the two sides were level, he ordered them to kindle a fire, and then to pour molten copper over it. Thus, by completing the barrier, he provided protection against the deception of Satan.\n\nAs Allah's promise, on the Day of Resurrection people will surge over one another in waves, the Trumpet (Sur) will be blown, and everyone will be gathered together. On that day, Hell will be displayed to the disbelievers; in this world, their eyes were veiled from Allah's signs/verses, so they did not see, nor could they bear to hear. These disbelievers took servants as allies and protectors instead of Allah; Hell has been prepared for them as hospitality.\n\nThose whose deeds are most in vain are those who strive in the life of this world thinking they are doing good deeds, but who disbelieve in the signs/verses of their Lord and in meeting Him. Therefore, their deeds have come to naught, and on the Day of Resurrection no weight or scale will be assigned to them. Their recompense is Hell because they disbelieved and took the signs/verses and the messengers in mockery.\n\nAs for those who believe and do righteous deeds, they will dwell eternally in the gardens of Firdaws, and they will not desire to depart from there. This is Allah's promise, and it will come to pass.\n\nThe words of Allah are boundless; even if the oceans were ink, the ocean would be exhausted before the words of my Lord are exhausted, even if the like of it were brought to replenish it. Our Prophet (peace be upon him) is but a human being; what is revealed to him is that your God is one God. Whoever hopes to meet his Lord, let him do righteous deeds and not associate anyone in the worship of his Lord. This is sincere servitude, free from ostentation (riya) and hidden polytheism (shirk).\n\nThis final section of Surah al-Kahf forms a solid foundation and a beautiful preparation for the transition to Surah Maryam."